{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 3, :comments ["<b>And this shall be the priests' due, etc.</b> Most of the commentators sought to find a way [to explain] the juxtaposition of this passage to the passage above, and they did not find a sufficient explanation; see in the book of Mahar[i]a, who brought many opinions on this. And I say: since it is written (I Samuel 8:11–14), ״זֶה יִהְיֶה מִשְׁפַּט הַמֶּלֶךְ אֲשֶׁר יִמְלֹךְ עֲלֵיכֶם וְגוֹ׳ וְאֶת שְׂדוֹתֵיכֶם וְכַרְמֵיכֶם יִקָּח״ and so forth — the word ״עֲלֵיכֶם״ (״over you״) hints that the judgment and deeds of the king were already alluded to in the Torah, as it is written: ״שׂוֹם תָּשִׂים עָלֶיךָ מֶלֶךְ״ — that he shall rule over your body, and all the more so over your property. Therefore [Scripture] juxtaposed the passage of the king, whose due is to take everything by force, whereas the priests' due is not so, but rather he takes the wage of his service, as it is written: ״לַעֲמֹד לְשָׁרֵת״ and so forth — not as Korah said, that they take by way of dominion like a king, as is explained in its place. And it may also be that it alluded to the king that he should not touch all that the priests have merited from the table on high, as it is written: וּמִנְדָּה בְלוֹ וַהֲלָךְ לָא שַׁלִּיט לְמִרְמֵא עֲלֵיהוֹן (Ezra 7:24)." "And the reason for the arm and the cheeks and the stomach — it is readily heard [understood] that the arm is the wage for the lifting of his hands, and the cheeks are the wage for the priestly blessing spoken with the mouth, and the stomach because through his merit HaShem sends the blessing such that one eats a little and is blessed in his intestines."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 18, :notes {0 "The Aramaic phrase from Ezra 7:24 is rendered in its original script as it appears; the citation conveys that a king has no authority to impose tax, tribute, or toll upon the priests."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:12:50.941202816Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}